(A name in braces inside a string prints its value there, and a colon
 after the name says how to lay the value out)

Print "Basic string interpolation".

A text called name is "Alice".
A number called age is 25.
Print "Hello, {name}! You are {age} years old.".

A text called city is "London".
Print "{name} lives in {city} and is {age}.".

Print "".
Print "Number formatting".

(A point and a count give the number of decimal places)
A float called pi is 3.14159265358979.
Print "Pi to 2 decimals: {pi:.2}".
Print "Pi to 4 decimals: {pi:.4}".
Print "Pi to 0 decimals: {pi:.0}".

(A width pads with spaces, or with zeros when it starts with 0)
A number called score is 42.
Print "Padded to 6 chars: [{score:6}]".
Print "Zero-padded: [{score:06}]".

Print "".
Print "Different number bases".

(Hexadecimal and octal always carry their 0x and 0o, and a width counts
 only the digits after it)
A number called value is 255.
Print "Decimal: {value}".
Print "Hexadecimal: {value:x}".
Print "Hex uppercase: {value:X}".
Print "Binary: {value:b}".
Print "Octal: {value:o}".

Print "With prefix: {value:x}".
Print "Padded hex: {value:04x}".

Print "".
Print "Escape sequences".

(Doubled braces print as braces, not as a value)
Print "Use {{braces}} for literal braces.".

Print "Tab:\there".
Print "Newline in string:\nSecond line".

Print "".
Print "Formatting expressions".

(Arithmetic works inside the braces too)
A number called x is 10.
A number called y is 3.
Print "x + y = {x add y}".
Print "x * y = {x multiply y}".

Print "Argument count: {arguments's count}".

Print "".
Print "In practice".

A float called price is 19.99.
Print "Total: ${price:.2}".

A float called ratio is 0.756.
Print "Progress: {ratio multiply 100:.1}%".
